  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved decent work employment opportunities through the development of coherent policies aligned to international standards on labour statistics in the Cook Islands</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO is supporting the development of the Cook Islands&apos; first-ever National Employment Policy (NEP)&#x2014; a coherent, comprehensive and integrated framework that promotes full and productive employment and decent work for all, in line with the SDGs. The NEP mainstreams employment in national policy-making and strives to achieve a dynamic and inclusive labour market by promoting job creation, enhancing skills development, and ensuring that employment opportunities are available and accessible to all.&#xa0;

The NEP draft is being finalized on the basis of an employment situation analysis as well as broad-based consultations and engagement with key stakeholders, including multiple government agencies, employers&apos; and workers&apos; organizations, and other relevant stakeholders. This collaborative and inclusive approach ensures that the NEP reflects stakeholders&apos; views, ensuring their support and commitment. The policy covers several key areas, such as youth employment, skills development, and job quality. By addressing these areas, the ILO aims to create a dynamic and inclusive labour market that benefits everyone in Cook Islands. The Government plans to adopt the NEP in mid-2025 and then move forward with its implementation, following the action plan and implementation mechanisms that form part of the NEP.

One critical component of this initiative is the improvement of labour statistics. The ILO has provided training and support to enhance the capacity of local institutions including in particular the National Statistics Office (NSO) and labour/employment ministries to collect, analyze, and utilize labour market data effectively, in line with the latest standards adopted at the International Conference of Labour Statisticians (ICLS). For Cook Islands specifically, the ILO provided technical support to a Labour Force Survey, for which data collection was completed in 2023. The LFS results are expected to be published by the NSO in mid-2025.</narrative>
  </description>
